<Project>

  <!--
    Package versions for the published packages. Every version here is a floor a consumer
    inherits: NuGet resolves the lowest version that satisfies the graph, so this is the
    oldest release a consumer may bring, not the newest tested. Each is the version that
    first carries the API the package actually calls, and moves only when new API is needed
    or an advisory forces it - .github/dependabot.yml deliberately does not watch this
    directory.

    The floors are self-checking rather than asserted: because restore takes the lowest
    applicable version, `dotnet test` compiles and runs against exactly these versions.

    Kept separate from the root file rather than merged into it so the two roles stay
    distinguishable by path - which is what lets .github/dependabot.yml scope itself to the
    test and sample projects without an id-by-id ignore list that silently rots.
  -->

  <!--
    MSBuild takes the *nearest* Directory.Packages.props and stops, so without this import
    the projects here would not see the root file at all - and MinVer, which
    Directory.Build.props adds to every project in the repository, would have no version.
    Importing keeps shared entries defined once instead of duplicated into both files.
  -->
  <Import Project="$([MSBuild]::GetPathOfFileAbove('Directory.Packages.props', '$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../'))" />

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Aspire.Hosting and Aspire.Hosting.JavaScript are one matched set - move them
         together. The reason is now historical rather than current, and worth writing down so
         the floor is not "corrected" back later: Aspire.Hosting.JavaScript 13.4.6 reaches into
         two of Aspire.Hosting's internal types across a friend-assembly boundary, and 13.5.x
         removes one of them and revokes access to the other. That pair restores and compiles
         clean and then throws TypeLoadException/MethodAccessException the first time a
         javascript service resolves, which is what #89 was.

         Read out of the assemblies, the coupling is gone as of 13.5.0 - checked at 13.5.0,
         13.5.1 and 13.5.2, each touching nothing non-public across 62 type references and 129
         member references - because Aspire closed it from its side, carrying its own copy of
         the helper. So above 13.5.0 a mismatched pair is ordinary API drift rather than a
         guaranteed crash.

         Only 13.5.0 has to be cleared, then; the floor sits at 13.5.2 because that is the
         version this repository builds and tests against, and a floor no one has run is worth
         less than one that is exercised on every CI run. Later patches still reach consumers
         on their own - this is a minimum, not a pin - so it does not need raising to stay
         current. They still ship in lockstep and are only tested that way, so there is nothing
         to gain by letting these two floors drift apart.

         The floor itself lives in $(AspireVersion) (Directory.Build.props) rather than as a
         literal here, even though this is otherwise the one file that states floors directly.
         That property is what aspire-matrix.yml overrides to rebuild the repo against another
         Aspire version, and it has to reach both of these lines at once for the pair to stay
         matched under an override.

         A NuGet floating version (AspireVersion=*, which is how that workflow's `latest` leg
         asks for the newest release) additionally needs CentralPackageFloatingVersionsEnabled:
         CPM rejects a floating PackageVersion outright with NU1011 otherwise, on the grounds
         that a floor pinned centrally and floating underneath every consumer is rarely what was
         meant. aspire-matrix.yml passes both; nothing else sets either, so an ordinary build
         here resolves the literal floor above. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Aspire.Hosting" Version="$(AspireVersion)" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Aspire.Hosting.JavaScript" Version="$(AspireVersion)" />

    <PackageVersion Include="CommunityToolkit.Aspire.Hosting.Java" Version="13.3.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="YamlDotNet" Version="18.1.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
